
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);
</code>

In this case, the code reads through the comments and output the related `Debug.Assert` statement. So, if the comment reads: "If the return value is non-negative, new list contains x", it outputs `Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);`. The assert statements will include the conditions mentioned in the comments.--------------------
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret is positive && New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
</code>


In the given code, a Debug.Assert was placed at the end of the method to ensure that `if the return value is non-negative, new list contains x`. The method checks whether new array list contains 'x' assuming that the return value is non-negative. We've added a check to make sure `New_Ret` (which is the return value) is greater than 0 and that `New_arrListContainsX` (which checks if array list still contains 'x') is true. If both conditions are met, the method will run as expected. If `New_Ret` is not greater than 0 or `New_arrListContainsX` is false, the Assertion will fail triggering a debug exception.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX == true);
</code>
The assert statement in this case checks that the return value is non-negative and that the new list contains x at the same time. The debug.assert is an example of .NET framework method, you will need to import it, in the namespace System.Diagnostics in order to use it, example: using System.Diagnostics;--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ==> New_arrListContainsX);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && (New_Ret < New_arrListCount - Old_arrListLastIndexOfX));
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);
</code>
This task is not possible to be solved by just parsing the comments in a C# script because assert statements have to be written in the code manually. Moreover, assertions can only be placed within methods in source code and cannot be added during runtime execution. You will have to manually place these assertions in the source code. Make sure your scripting language (like Python, JavaScript) has good libraries and frameworks that perform this task or integrate directly the debugger in your IDE.--------------------
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_x = x;
        int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

		Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);	
    }
}
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? New_arrListContainsX : !New_arrListContainsX);
</code>

The reason we are writing Debug.Assert is because, in this specific situation, we are expecting if the return value is non-negative, then we would expect the array list to contain 'x'. So, we are checking if array list contains 'x' when New_Ret is non-negative and vice versa, hence we are using 'new_arrListContainsX' inside the Debug.Assert. If there's no return value (which shouldn't come into play here), we are asserting that the list won't contain 'x'.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX);
</code>

